  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=1$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,2].$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ except at the point $x=1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x)=3,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"",
